@node Installation
@chapter Installation

PPS Mode requires GNU Emacs 29.1 or newer.  Emacs 29 and later can install
the stable branch directly from GitHub.  Evaluate:

@lisp
(package-vc-install
 '(pps-mode
   :url "https://github.com/Pinnacle-Point-Development/pps-mode.git"
   :vc-backend Git
   :main-file "pps-mode.el"))
@end lisp

For a checkout, add its directory to @code{load-path} and load the package:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/pps-mode")
(require 'pps-mode)
@end lisp

Files whose names end in @file{.pps} then open in PPS Mode automatically.
Use @kbd{M-x pps-info} to open this manual.  It is also available from the
Info directory with @kbd{C-h i m PPS Mode RET}.

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

Visit an existing @file{.pps} file with @kbd{C-x C-f}.  To make a workbook,
visit a new filename ending in @file{.pps}; PPS Mode creates one blank sheet.

The current cell has a highlighted border.  Press @kbd{RET}, @kbd{F2}, or
@kbd{e} to edit it.  Enter plain text, a number, a boolean, a date, or a
formula as appropriate for its column type.  Save with the usual @kbd{C-x
C-s}.  Normal Emacs backups, file locking, auto-save, and revert apply.

Press @kbd{?} for mode help and use the @samp{Spreadsheet} menu to discover
all commands.

@node Navigation
@chapter Navigation

Arrow keys and @kbd{h}, @kbd{j}, @kbd{k}, and @kbd{l} move one cell.
@kbd{TAB} and @kbd{S-TAB} move across columns and wrap between rows.
@kbd{M-<} and @kbd{M->} move to the first and last row.  @kbd{Home} and
@kbd{End} move to the first and last column.  Page Up and Page Down move by a
rendered page.

Use @kbd{g} to enter an A1 address such as @samp{B12}, or a sheet-qualified
address such as @samp{Rates!B2}.  @kbd{[} and @kbd{]} select the previous and
next sheet.

@node Editing
@chapter Editing

@section Cells and selections

Press @kbd{SPC} on one corner, move to the opposite corner, and run a command
to act on the rectangular selection.  Press @kbd{SPC} again or @kbd{C-g} to
clear the mark.  @kbd{DEL} clears selected cells.  @kbd{F} fills every
selected cell from the top-left cell.

@kbd{M-w}, @kbd{C-w}, and @kbd{C-y} copy, cut, and paste rectangles.  PPS Mode
uses tab-separated text on the kill ring, so copied data can move between the
workbook and other programs.  Formula references adjust when formula cells
are copied.

@section Rows and columns

@kbd{o} inserts a row below the current row and @kbd{O} inserts one above.
@kbd{D} deletes selected rows.  @kbd{I} inserts a column before the current
one.  The @samp{Rows and Columns} menu also inserts a column after the current
one and changes column names, types, and display widths.

Structural changes update formula references.  A reference to a deleted cell
becomes a @samp{#REF!} error.  Use @kbd{u} and @kbd{U} for logical undo and
redo within the workbook.

@node Formulas
@chapter Formulas

Press @kbd{=} to enter a formula.  Formula syntax resembles Lisp, with cell
references written as A1 addresses:

@example
=(+ B1 C1)
=(sum (range B1 B20))
=(if (> C1 0) "OPEN" "CLOSED")
=(+ (cell "Rates" B2) $A$1)
@end example

Supported operations include arithmetic, comparison, logic, text functions,
numeric helpers, aggregates, cell references, and ranges.  The reader checks
every formula against a fixed allowlist.  Formulas cannot evaluate arbitrary
Lisp or access files, processes, or the network.

Dollar signs make row or column components absolute when a formula is copied.
For example, @samp{$A1} fixes column A and @samp{A$1} fixes row 1.  Use
@kbd{C-c =} to clear the formula cache and recalculate the workbook.

@node Sheets
@chapter Sheets

Use @kbd{C-c s} to switch by name, @kbd{C-c a} to add a sheet, and @kbd{C-c
n} to rename one.  @kbd{C-c d} duplicates the active sheet and @kbd{C-c x}
deletes it.  A workbook always contains at least one sheet, and sheet names
are unique and case-sensitive.

@node Data Tools
@chapter Data Tools

@kbd{/} searches for a regular expression.  @kbd{f} filters visible rows;
@kbd{C-c C-f} clears the filter.  @kbd{s} sorts rows by the active column.
With a prefix argument, it sorts in descending order.

@kbd{C-c +} reports the count and numeric summary of a selection.  @kbd{C-c
?} describes the source and calculated value of the current cell.  @kbd{C-c
v} validates column types and reports formula errors.

@code{pps-import-delimited-file} imports CSV or TSV into a new sheet.
@code{pps-export-delimited-file} exports the active sheet.  Prefix arguments
enable type inference during import and calculated values during export.

@node File Format
@chapter File Format

A PPS file is UTF-8 text containing one data-only S-expression.  Its root is
a @code{pps} form with format version 1, metadata, an active sheet name, and a
vector of sheets.  Each sheet contains column descriptions and row vectors.
Cells can hold blanks, booleans, strings, numbers, dates, date-times, or a
validated formula tree.

The reader never passes workbook data to @code{eval}.  It rejects trailing
forms, unknown properties, invalid cell forms, excessive dimensions, and
unrecognized formula operators.  The complete normative contract is in
@file{FORMAT.md} in the distribution.

@node Customization
@chapter Customization

Use @kbd{M-x customize-group RET pps RET} to change PPS Mode settings.

@table @code
@item pps-retro-display
Use the black and yellow COLORMODECTL inspired display.
@item pps-use-unicode-borders
Draw the grid with Unicode box characters.
@item pps-default-column-width
Set the default width of columns that have no stored width.
@item pps-page-size
Set the number of rows rendered at once.
@item pps-history-limit
Limit the logical workbook undo history.
@item pps-max-file-bytes
Limit accepted input size.
@item pps-max-rows
Limit rows per sheet.
@item pps-max-columns
Limit columns per sheet.
@item pps-max-formula-range-cells
Limit cells expanded by one formula range.
@end table

